    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Romanian principality founded in the mid-14th century between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a>, independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from about 1359.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen the Great</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vaslui">Vaslui</a> in 1475, but Moldavia became an Ottoman tributary and was ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phanariotes">Phanariots</a> 1711–1821.</li><li>It lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and in 1859 united with Wallachia to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> called Moldavia Kara Boğdan, “Black Bogdania”, after its founding prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogdan-i-of-moldavia">Bogdan I</a>, and the name survived in Turkish for centuries.</p></aside>
    </section>

