    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Louis I of Hungary" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_I_(Chronicon_Pictum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Márk Kálti, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Moldavia took shape in the mid-fourteenth century in the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-carpathians">Eastern Carpathians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a>, recently freed from the control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. According to the traditional account, around 1346–1347 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dragos-voivode-of-moldavia">Dragoș</a>, a Romanian noble from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maramures">Maramureș</a>, across the mountains to found a frontier march on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova-river">Moldova River</a>; the exact date and circumstances are uncertain, and the tradition of his “dismounting” (<em>descălecat</em>) was elaborated later. Around 1359 another Maramureș noble, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogdan-i-of-moldavia">Bogdan I</a>, who had quarrelled with the Hungarian crown, crossed into Moldavia, drove out Dragoș’s descendants and resisted Hungarian attempts to reimpose control, so the principality became independent; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> later called it <em>Kara Boğdan</em>, “Black Bogdania”, after him. His successors, Lațcu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petru-i-of-moldavia">Petru I Mușat</a>, built the institutions of a state: Lațcu sought a Catholic bishopric at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siret">Siret</a> around 1371, while Petru I struck coins, did homage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> in 1387 to balance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and established the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a>. By about 1400 the principality reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> at the mouth of the Dniester, where the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Cetatea Albă</a> (Akkerman) lay, and in the early fifteenth century it also gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiliya">Chilia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> delta.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/suceava"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suceava.jpg" alt="Suceava" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palatul_Administrativ_din_Suceava_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rosenborg BK Fan, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Moldavian society was overwhelmingly rural. Boyars, the landed nobility, held estates worked by dependent peasants, while free peasant communities known as <em>răzeși</em> kept their own land, especially in the hills and the centre of the country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a> were held as slaves by the prince, the monasteries and the boyars until emancipation in 1855–1856. The country raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, horses, sheep and pigs, and exported livestock, honey, wax, hides and, increasingly, grain. Its early wealth also came from transit: the “Moldavian road” carried goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiliya">Chilia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Cetatea Albă</a>, where Genoese and Levantine merchants traded. Towns such as Suceava, Iași, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baia-romania">Baia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siret">Siret</a> held German, Armenian and Jewish merchant communities alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>, using Church Slavonic in its liturgy and chancery until the seventeenth century, was the main patron of culture, and monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/putna-monastery">Putna</a>, Neamț and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucevita-monastery">Sucevița</a> were centres of learning, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> copying and embroidery. After the coastal ports fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1484 the economy turned inwards, and from the sixteenth century the sultans demanded grain, livestock and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> at fixed prices, a burden that grew heavy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phanariotes">Phanariot</a> period.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vaslui"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-vaslui.jpg" alt="Battle of Vaslui" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vaslui">Battle of Vaslui</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaslui_15cMD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anonimu at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Moldavia relied on the princely retinue, the boyars’ followers and, in emergencies, the “great host”, a levy in which free peasants were called to arms; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> and light cavalry dominated. Its strength rested on fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a>, Neamț, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khotyn">Hotin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiliya">Chilia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Cetatea Albă</a>, and on the forested, broken terrain that favoured ambush. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen the Great</a> (r. 1457–1504) made the most of these assets. He took Chilia in 1465, repulsed the Hungarian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baia-romania">Baia</a> in 1467, and on 10 January 1475 destroyed a much larger Ottoman army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadim-suleiman-pasha">Hadım Suleiman Pasha</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vaslui">Battle of Vaslui</a>, fought in fog near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barlad-river">Bârlad River</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> himself invaded in 1476 and defeated Stephen at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-valea-alba">Valea Albă</a> (Războieni), but failed to take Suceava or Neamț and withdrew. In 1484 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Sultan Bayezid II</a> captured Chilia and Cetatea Albă, cutting Moldavia off from the sea, and in 1497 Stephen beat a Polish invasion under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-albert">John I Albert</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-cosmin-forest">Cosmin Forest</a>. Later princes had less room: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petru-rares">Petru Rareș</a> lost his throne and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bender-moldova">Tighina</a> (Bender) region when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> invaded in 1538, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimitrie-cantemir">Dimitrie Cantemir</a>’s alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> ended in the Russian defeat on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Pruth</a> in 1711.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/regulamentul-organic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/regulamentul-organic.jpg" alt="Regulamentul Organic" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/regulamentul-organic">Regulamentul Organic</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wallachian_Regulamentul_organic_1832.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The prince, styled voivode or hospodar, held wide powers as military commander, supreme judge and owner of unclaimed land, but he was chosen from the male descendants of earlier princes, legitimate or not, and acclaimed by the boyars and the Church, so successions were often violent. He ruled with a princely council of great office-holders such as the <em>logofăt</em> (chancellor), <em>vornic</em> (justice and the court), <em>hatman</em> (army) and <em>vistiernic</em> (treasury). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-good">Alexander the Good</a> (r. 1400–1432) organised the chancery, the fiscal system and the Church; in 1401 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecumenical-patriarch-of-constantinople">Patriarch of Constantinople</a> recognised a Moldavian metropolitan see at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a>. Law was largely customary until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasile-lupu">Vasile Lupu</a> (r. 1634–1653) issued the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carte-romaneasca-de-invatatura">Carte românească de învățătură</a></em> of 1646, one of the first law codes printed in Romanian. The capital moved from Suceava to Iași in 1564. Ottoman suzerainty, accepted with tribute from 1456 and tightened after 1538, meant that princes were confirmed and increasingly appointed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sublime-porte">Porte</a>. From 1711 to 1821 the sultans named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phanariotes">Phanariot Greek</a> princes, often for short terms, some of whom, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-mavrocordatos">Constantine Mavrocordatos</a>, abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in 1749 and reformed taxation. After 1829 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regulamentul-organic">Organic Regulation</a> of 1832, introduced under Russian protection, created a modern administration and an assembly of boyars.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/voronet-monastery"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/voronet-monastery.jpg" alt="Voroneț Monastery" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/voronet-monastery">Voroneț Monastery</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Voronet_Intrare.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dstefanescu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Moldavia’s deepest legacy is cultural. The churches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen the Great</a> and his successors, and especially the sixteenth-century monasteries whose outer walls were covered with frescoes under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petru-rares">Petru Rareș</a> and later princes, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voronet-monastery">Voroneț</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humor-monastery">Humor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldovita-monastery">Moldovița</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbore-church">Arbore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucevita-monastery">Sucevița</a>, are a group on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage List</a>. Moldavian chroniclers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigore-ureche">Grigore Ureche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miron-costin">Miron Costin</a> wrote some of the first histories in Romanian and argued for the Roman origin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>; Metropolitan Dosoftei translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Psalms</a> into Romanian verse. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimitrie-cantemir">Dimitrie Cantemir</a>, prince in 1693 and 1710–1711 and later a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-academy-of-sciences">Berlin Academy</a>, wrote the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/descriptio-moldaviae">Descriptio Moldaviae</a></em> and a history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> widely read in eighteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Politically, Stephen the Great’s resistance to the Ottomans made him a national hero, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> praised him as an “athlete of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Christ</a>”. The principality’s territory now lies in three countries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812, became in 1991 the core of the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Republic of Moldova</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> and southern Bessarabia belong to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and western Moldavia forms the north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, where Iași remains a leading university city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crimean-war.jpg" alt="Crimean War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Simpson_-_Attack_on_the_Malakoff.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William Simpson, artist (1823-1899) E[dmond]. Morin, lithographer (1824 - 1882), working for &quot;Day &amp;amp; Son, lithrs. ..., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Moldavia never collapsed; it was steadily reduced and finally merged. Ottoman control tightened after 1538, and the chronic contest among boyar factions and claimants, each seeking support in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, wore down the principality’s autonomy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-the-brave">Michael the Brave of Wallachia</a> held it briefly in 1600. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimitrie-cantemir">Cantemir</a>’s defection in 1711 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sublime-porte">Porte</a> imposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phanariotes">Phanariot</a> regime, and the wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> made the country a repeated battlefield. In 1775 Austria annexed the north-west, which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a>, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bucharest-1812">Treaty of Bucharest</a> of 1812 Russia took everything east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Pruth</a>, nearly half the principality. The Greek rising of 1821, which began at Iași, ended Phanariot rule, and native princes returned in 1822. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-adrianople-1829">Treaty of Adrianople</a> of 1829 placed Moldavia and Wallachia under a Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> while leaving them under Ottoman suzerainty. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1856">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1856 returned southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> and placed the principalities under a guarantee of the great powers, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-convention-of-1858">Paris Convention of 1858</a> allowed separate princes and assemblies. Both assemblies elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandru-ioan-cuza">Alexandru Ioan Cuza</a> in January 1859, in Moldavia on 5 January, creating the personal union that was formally recognised as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> in 1862.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Principality of Moldavia was one of the two medieval Romanian states; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen the Great</a> it resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, its painted monasteries are among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s finest medieval art, and its union with Wallachia in 1859 created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, while its eastern half became today’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Republic of Moldova</a>.</em></p>

